text:
marta: Hi all, opened a tiny Next.js starter that demos streaming chat + SSR fallback. When running on the new edge runtime the SDK's streaming helper doesn't work the same as Node — the sample stalls during hydration and the stream never closes. Repo: https://github.com/redwood-inference/next-stream-ssr-starter

tom: can you paste the simplified server code and the client-side hydrate code? also which SDK version?

marta: SDK v0.9.2. Server code (API route deployed to edge runtime):
```js
// pages/api/stream.js
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/sdk'
export const runtime = 'edge'
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY })
export default async function (req) {
  const stream = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'l2-stream', input: 'hello' })
  return new Response(stream.body, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream' } })
}
```

Client hydration code:
```js
// components/ChatStream.jsx
useEffect(()=>{
  const s = new EventSource('/api/stream')
  s.onmessage = (e)=> setChunks(c=>c+e.data)
  s.onerror = ()=> setError(true)
},[])
```

ashwin: interesting. On Node the SDK returns a ReadableStream that already emits SSE-compatible chunks; on Edge the runtime ReadableStream semantics differ and the SDK currently wraps differently (we noticed this in internal tests). The mismatch can cause the stream to never flush because TextEncoderStream isn't applied the same way.

sophie: two fixes: 1) for the starter, fallback to a polling endpoint for SSR hydration to avoid fragile streaming on edge, or 2) patch the SDK instantiation for edge to use the stream adapter: `stream: { adapter: 'edge-sse' }` (we added this experimental option in v0.9.3). Also ensure the response sets `cache: 'no-store'` for streaming.

marta: tried `adapter: 'edge-sse'` locally — got a nicer chunk flow but still saw a lingering connection in some browsers. server logs show repeated small reads and the SDK retry wrapper kicks in on aborted reads. snippet of logs: ```edge: stream reader closed unexpectedly
redwood-sdk-js retrying request (attempt 2)```

nashwin: the retry on aborted stream is a bug — the SDK should not retry streaming reads that fail once the response has started. I'll open a bug. For the starter, your safest short-term change is: convert the API to an incremental HTML SSR route (Next streaming SSR) that uses server-sent chunks to hydrate progressively instead of raw EventSource. Example: return `new Response(readable, { headers:{ 'Content-Type':'text/html; charset=utf-8' }})` and stream the partial HTML. That avoids the SDK retry logic entirely since the response lifecycle is HTML.

sophie: also ensure you set an explicit timeout/AbortController when calling client.chat.stream so aborted edges don't cause partial retries. Example: ```js
const ac = new AbortController();
setTimeout(()=>ac.abort(), 30_000);
const stream = await client.chat.stream({ input, signal: ac.signal })
```

marta: thanks. I'll make the quick change to progressive HTML streaming in the starter and add a note in the README about edge stream parity + recommended SDK version (>=0.9.3). I'll also add a tiny CI smoke test that hits the stream endpoint and asserts the first chunk arrives within 5s, otherwise fail.

nashwin: opened issue RDW-1218: "SDK should not retry after streaming response body has started; handle edge readable streams parity". I'll push a patch to v0.9.4 to add `adapter: 'edge-sse'` by default when runtime=== 'edge' and fix the retry logic.

devex-bot: Created PR #45 in next-stream-ssr-starter: "fallback to progressive HTML streaming on edge, add CI smoke test, README notes about SDK stream adapter"

marta: PR up now: https://github.com/redwood-inference/next-stream-ssr-starter/pull/5 — thanks for the fast triage. Closing loop once CI finishes.
